<%# The full waterfall. Local: `presenter` (DistributionPresenter). See the UI design spec §5.

    Rendered only when something is short, and then in full with no collapse — the density is
    the data's decision, not a setting's.

    The cutoff marks where the cash ran out, which is AFTER the last envelope that received any
    of it: a pool funded $355 of $400 did get money. So the line goes before the first envelope
    that got nothing, or at the end when the money ran out inside the last row. Same rule and
    the same reasoning as Home's waterfall; there is no multi-account caveat here, because a
    distribution is scoped to one account by definition. %>
<% rows = presenter.lines %>
<%# `nil` unless something is actually short. With every envelope funded the index finds nothing,
    falls back to the row count and draws "ran out here · $0.00 unfunded" under the last row of a
    screen where nothing ran out — which is exactly what this table now renders whenever an
    overdue bill opened it on a covered period. Same guard, and the same reason, as Home's. %>
<%# Read off the SAME fill as everything else on this card. An override re-runs the waterfall
    below it, so the money genuinely does reach further down when a high row is cut — a cutoff
    computed against the un-overridden proposal would sit above envelopes the edit had just
    funded, and the unfunded total beside it would refuse to move while the buffer grew. %>
<% cutoff = presenter.short? ? (rows.index { |line| line.funded.zero? && line.short? } || rows.length) : nil %>

<section id="distribution-waterfall" class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" aria-labelledby="waterfall-heading">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 id="waterfall-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Where your money goes</h3>
    <%# Why the table is open, in its own words. The two reasons are different problems with
        different fixes — money missing, versus money present and a bill unpaid — and a header
        that only ever named the first would leave the overdue screen looking like a mistake. %>
    <% if presenter.short? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger">
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> of what your envelopes asked for isn't there.
        Every row is shown, in the order the money reaches them.
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600">
        Every envelope gets what it asked for, but something below still needs you — so the whole
        split is shown rather than a single line.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# AMENDMENT D / SPEC §5, IN WORDS ON THE SCREEN. An override and a rule change are easy to
        confuse and must not look alike: one is "put in $200 this once", the other changes every
        future period and happens somewhere else entirely. The box below looks like a settings
        field, so the screen has to say which of the two it is before the user types in it —
        and it says so whether or not anything has been edited yet, because the moment it
        matters is the moment before the first edit. %>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 px-3 py-2 text-xs text-gray-600">
      Every amount below is editable.
      <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">An edit here is a one-off for this distribution
      only — it does not change the rule.</span>
      The envelope goes on asking for the same amount every period; what you leave out is pushed
      onto the periods that are left. To change the rule itself, edit the envelope's budget.
    </p>

    <%# Said once, above the table, when something HAS been edited. Each edited row carries its
        own consequence line, but a row whose consequence is silent (a rate envelope, a dateless
        goal) would otherwise come back from the reload looking untouched. %>
    <% if presenter.overridden? %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-status-warning" id="distribution-override-note">
        Showing your edits. Nothing has been saved — confirming is what writes the split.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# WHERE THE MONEY WENT, once. With TWO OR MORE rows edited this replaces the per-row
        destination sentences entirely, because per-row answers are each true against their own
        counterfactual and do not sum — they overstate the buffer and, worse, silently miss a row
        that either edit alone would have funded. One decomposition against the untouched
        proposal names every recipient exactly once and adds up. With one edit it is nil and the
        row carries its own sentence instead. See DistributionPresenter::Redirect. %>
    <% if presenter.redirect %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600" id="distribution-redirect">
        <%= distribution_redirect_sentence(presenter.redirect) %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# Red pools with no row of their own: an overdue bill whose envelope already holds the money
      asks for nothing and is rejected from the waterfall, so this band is the only place on the
      screen it can appear. It is also the thing the density switch fired on, and a table that
      opened without saying why reads as a bug. %>
  <% presenter.alerts.each do |pool, standing| %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 bg-status-danger-light" data-alert-pool="<%= pool.name %>">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= pool.name %></span>
        <span class="text-sm text-status-danger"><%= pool_status_label(standing) %></span>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-gray-600">
        Its money is already there — this distribution has nothing to add.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ONE FORM ROUND THE WHOLE TABLE, and it is a GET back to this same screen. The consequence
      of an override is a real recomputation over the ledger — periods_until_due, the sweep the
      confirm will take, the pool's own PoolStatus — so it is answered by the server that owns
      those readers rather than re-derived in JavaScript, where it would be a second, silently
      divergent copy of the schedule maths.

      NOTHING HERE WRITES (amendment E): the override lives in the query string and in these
      boxes until Task 6's confirm submits the same fields to `create`. The field names are
      `overrides[<pool id>]` because that is exactly what AllocationCommitter consumes. %>
  <%= form_with url: new_distribution_path, method: :get do %>
    <%# Without this the reload falls back to "the account the pay landed in" and an override
        typed on a second account would come back describing the first one. %>
    <%= hidden_field_tag :account_id, presenter.account.id %>
    <%# Carried forward only when the user asked for the table. Without it, clearing the last
        edit on a comfortable period collapses the table out from under the boxes mid-edit. %>
    <%= hidden_field_tag :expand, 1 if presenter.expand_requested? %>

    <ol>
      <% rows.each_with_index do |line, index| %>
        <%= render "cutoff", presenter: presenter if index == cutoff %>
        <li class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100" data-pool-name="<%= line.pool.name %>">
          <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
            <span class="text-gray-900">
              <span class="text-xs text-gray-400"><%= index + 1 %></span>
              <span class="font-medium"><%= line.pool.name %></span>
            </span>
            <span class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <%# The readout keeps its place and its colour: the box holds one number, and
                  `$200.00 of $500.00` is the half of the sentence a bare box cannot carry. %>
              <span data-row-amount="<%= line.pool.name %>"
                    class="tabular-nums text-sm <%= line.short? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
                <%= distribution_line_amount(line) %>
              </span>
              <label class="sr-only" for="override-<%= line.pool.id %>">Amount for <%= line.pool.name %></label>
              <%# EMPTY UNLESS THE USER TYPED HERE, with the proposal as the placeholder, and that
                  is not a styling choice. A box pre-filled with the proposal submits a figure for
                  every row, and since the fill now treats a submitted figure as that row's ask,
                  every untouched row would be pinned at its old amount and money freed above it
                  could never reach anything. Empty means "I did not touch this"; clearing a box
                  is therefore also how you undo an edit, which is the mockup's
                  "Reset Rent to $500" for free.

                  `min: 0` refuses a negative before it is submitted. A negative is bad input
                  rather than a shape with a meaning — AllocationCalculator#row_for deliberately
                  carries it through to fail PoolMovement's validation loudly, which is the right
                  behaviour for a commit and the wrong one for a box you are still typing in. %>
              <%= number_field_tag "overrides[#{line.pool.id}]",
                                   distribution_override_value(line),
                                   id: "override-#{line.pool.id}",
                                   step: "0.01",
                                   min: "0",
                                   placeholder: distribution_override_placeholder(line),
                                   class: class_names("form-input w-28 py-1 text-right tabular-nums",
                                                      "border-status-warning" => line.overridden?) %>
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-gray-500">
            <%= distribution_line_detail(line) %>
            <% if line.swept? %>· <%= number_to_currency(line.swept) %> swept back<% end %>
          </p>
          <%# WHERE THE MONEY WENT, said on the row that moved it and nowhere else. The cascade
              re-fills every envelope below an edit, so rows nobody touched change their figures;
              this is the sentence that keeps that from being money moving for reasons the screen
              does not state. NOW first, then the consequence's LATER — that is the order the two
              questions arrive in. Grey, because it is a statement of fact rather than a caution. %>
          <% if line.redirect %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500" data-redirect="<%= line.pool.name %>">
              ↳ <%= distribution_redirect_sentence(line.redirect) %>
            </p>
          <% end %>
          <%# Only where the per-period ask actually moved (spec §5). A row edited in a way that
              changes nothing downstream — a dateless goal, a rate envelope whose leftover is
              swept and topped back up either way — says nothing, because there is nothing to
              say. See DistributionPresenter::Consequence#worth_saying?. %>
          <% if line.consequence %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs <%= line.consequence.unrecoverable? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-status-warning" %>"
               data-consequence="<%= line.pool.name %>">
              ↳ <%= distribution_consequence_sentence(line.consequence) %>
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </li>
      <% end %>
      <%# The money ran out INSIDE the last row — every envelope got something, one of them not
          enough — so there is no row to draw the line above and it belongs at the end. %>
      <%= render "cutoff", presenter: presenter if cutoff == rows.length %>
    </ol>

    <%# The buffer line, the waterfall's last row: what is left once every envelope above has
        taken what it could. Two moments, arrow between them, because the LEVEL matters less than
        the direction (spec §7.1) — a buffer falling every period is the slow bleed this line
        exists to catch. Negative is a true statement about an account in trouble and is printed
        as one rather than floored at zero.

        INSIDE the form, and it moves with the boxes above it: money an override leaves out of an
        envelope does not evaporate, it stays here, and money an override adds comes from here.
        That is `Σ pools == your bank balance` shown before it is written. %>
    <div id="distribution-buffer" class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-3 bg-gray-50">
      <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">
        Stays in buffer<span class="text-gray-500"><%= buffer_target_clause(presenter) %></span>
      </span>
      <span class="tabular-nums text-sm <%= presenter.leftover.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.buffer_carried) %> → <%= number_to_currency(presenter.leftover) %>
      </span>
    </div>

    <%# The recompute. An explicit button rather than a submit-on-blur: the figures below change
        as a result of pressing it, and a page that re-renders under the cursor while the user is
        still deciding is the shape that makes people distrust a screen about their money.
        Task 6's confirm goes to the LEFT of this, as the primary action. %>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 border-t border-gray-200 px-6 py-4">
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Edits are applied when you confirm — nothing has moved yet.</p>
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Update figures</button>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
